The "Debug.Assert" statement checks whether the condition specified in it is true. If the condition is not true, it triggers an Assertion in the form of an System.Diagnostics.Assert.AssertionFailedException.
In this case, if the value of "New_Ret" is negative, meaning "arrList.IndexOf(x)" doesn't find "x" in the list, then it fails to assert. The message "If the return value is negative, list count remains same" appears when the assertion fails, giving you a helpful message. This way, you can keep track of what exactly is failing the test when there is an error or a bug in your code.--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount : true);

        }
    }
}
```
This C# solution captures all the logic written in the PUT_IndexOfContract() method comments and translates it to C# code. Now, if the assertion condition is not met, the program will break in debug mode, reporting a Debug Assertion Failed error message on the IDE. It is also important to note that in order to activate the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute, you need to use Pex libraries as well as some other packages.--------------------The task is to generate unit tests for given source code. You can use NUnit for this purpose. Here is an example how you can generate NUnit tests for the given code:

```cs
using NUnit.Framework;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [Test]
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract_IsTrue([Values(-100, -1, 0, 1, 100)]int x)
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();          
            arrList.Add(x);

            // Assume Precondition
            Assume.That(x >= -2147483648 && x <= 2147483647);
            
            // Inputs
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);
            
            // Action
            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            arrList.Remove(x);
            
            // Outputs
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Assertion
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "$old_arrList_Count");
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x");
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX, "$new_arrList_contains_x");
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "If the return value is negative, list count remains same");
        }
    }
}
```
This test generates unit tests for the methods. It contains a [Test method] using NUnit's [Test] attribute and parameterized using [Values] attribute. The assumption checks if `x` is within the integer range and that `x` is added to the ArrayList. If those conditions are met, it will generate expected output by calling the method and making assertions against it. It is also a good idea to remove `x` from the ArrayList after verification to ensure it doesn't affect future tests.--------------------Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 == (Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount));

The above `Debug.Assert` statement checks if the `IndexOf` method's return value is negative (which means that the item was not found), then it checks if the list's count remains same before and after the operation if the return value is negative. If the list count remains the same, it means the operation did not change its count.--------------------The output for the provided csharp code using Debug.Assert statements could look like this:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "If the return value is negative, list count remains same");
```

Please note that you should replace the assert line with specific values captured in the pre-conditions section.

This will check if the `IndexOf` method returns a negative value (indicating that the element does not exist in the list), but also check that the count of the list remains the same (`New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount`). The message "If the return value is negative, list count remains same" explains what the assertion is checking. Replace it with a more descriptive message if you wish.--------------------```cs

using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "If the return value is negative, list count remains same");
        }
    }
}

```
This edited code will generate an assertion, with the message "If the return value is negative, list count remains same", if New_Ret is less than 0 or if Old_arrListCount equals New_arrListCount. This assumes that New_Ret and Old_arrListCount always have values in the if-condition.--------------------Here is the program. It scans the comments starting with `@@@` and if the comment has a description of a specific condition/action, it makes a corresponding Debug.Assert statement.

